BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:As American as Apple Pie: New England’s Historic Food and Drink
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTSTAMP:20260924T152951Z
UID:d42df59944de7e3e7eb7b481ddd3@visitri-calendar
DESCRIPTION:Taste and discover the history of early New England food on Th
 ursday\, September 24th at 6pm\, at “As American as Apple Pie: New Engla
 nd’s Historic Food and Drink\,” featuring a talk from food historians 
 Keith Stavely &amp\; Kathleen Fitzgerald\, as well as cider\, pie\, and jo
 hnnycakes from local providers. Stavely &amp\; Fitzgerald will present on 
 their books\, America’s Founding Food: The Story of New England Cooking
 \, and the forthcoming Northern Comfort: The New England Baking Tradition.
  Discover the history of New England foodways\, including the importance o
 f cider in New England farming\, regional breads and pies\, and the ninete
 enth-century raconteur who promoted the idea that the cooking—especially
  the baking—of southern Rhode Island was easily the best to be found any
 where. In addition to the talk\, guests will receive a glass of traditiona
 lly produced cider made with historic apple varietals from Sowam’s Cider
  Works\, a personal apple pie made with Rhode Island greenings from Babes 
 Pie Co.\, and samples of Jonnycakes made on site by Kenyon’s Grist Mill.
  Jamestown\, RI\, authors Keith Stavely and Kathleen Fitzgerald made their
  debut with America’s Founding Food: The Story of New England Cooking in
  2004. It was deemed a “classic tome” (Choice) and was awarded a 2005 
 Best of the Best from University Presses selection by the American Associa
 tion of University Presses. Their other books include the award-winning Un
 ited Tastes: The Making of the First American Cookbook (2017) and the accl
 aimed Northern Hospitality: Cooking by the Book in New England (2011). Sta
 vely and Fitzgerald have spoken at international conferences\, historical 
 societies\, libraries\, museums\, and to a variety of community and profes
 sional groups\, bringing the hidden history and cultural significance of N
 ew England and early American foodways to light. The latest book\, Norther
 n Comfort: The New England Baking Tradition\, will be out this November fr
 om New York University Press. Tickets are $35 for RIHS Members and $40 for
  Non-Members. Guests can register here.\n\nSource: https://www.visitrhodei
 sland.com/event/as-american-as-apple-pie%3a-new-england%e2%80%99s-historic
 -food-and-drink/111529/
LOCATION:Rhode Island Historical Society\, Aldrich House\, 110 Benevolent 
 Street\, Providence\, RI 02906 Time: 6:00 PM to 7:00 PM
URL:https://www.visitrhodeisland.com/event/as-american-as-apple-pie%3a-new
 -england%e2%80%99s-historic-food-and-drink/111529/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Perfect Arrangement at the Barker Playhouse
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTSTAMP:20260924T152951Z
UID:4b6ac70b428ab7851529626748e9@visitri-calendar
DESCRIPTION:Join the Rhode Island Historical Society on Thursday\, October
  1 for the Barker Playhouse’s production of Perfect Arrangement. It’s 
 1950\, and new colors are being added to the Red Scare. Two U.S. State Dep
 artment employees\, Bob and Norma\, have been tasked with identifying sexu
 al deviants within their ranks. There’s just one problem: both Bob and N
 orma are gay\, and have married each other’s partners as a carefully con
 structed cover. Inspired by the true story of the earliest stirrings of th
 e American gay rights movement\, madcap classic sitcom-style laughs give w
 ay to provocative drama as two “All American” couples are forced to st
 are down the closed door. Prior to the 7:30 p.m. performance\, guests will
  be treated to light refreshments. Doors open at 6:45 p.m.\n\nSource: http
 s://www.visitrhodeisland.com/event/perfect-arrangement-at-the-barker-playh
 ouse/111656/
LOCATION:The Barker Playhouse\, 400 Benefit Street\, Providence\, RI 02903
  Time: 7:30 PM to 9:00 PM 400 Benefit Street\, Providence\, RI 02903 OFFIC
